# agent worktrees (transient, created by the ticket pipeline)
#
# Both conventions in use, and the second one is the point (MUSE-116). `.claude/worktrees/`
# is what the pipeline creates; `.worktrees/` is where per-ticket and QA worktrees are added
# by hand. `.worktrees/` was missing here, and several gates take their exclusions from this
# file — `test/seo.test.ts`'s source scan and `scripts/check-format.mjs` — so `npm test` run
# from the main checkout failed whenever a `.worktrees/<name>/` existed: seven files inside
# one of them named the deploy host at paths that are not `test/seo.test.ts`'s three
# exemptions. Invisible from inside a worktree, where the scan root is the worktree itself,
# and impossible in CI, which checks out fresh.
#
# **Why excluding this from an otherwise exhaustive scan is safe, which is the question the
# next person will have.** `test/seo.test.ts`'s host scan takes its exclusions from here
# precisely so there is no scan list to keep current, and an entry here is therefore also an
# exemption from it — the shape MUSE-45 warns about ("a skip list is MUSE-19's allow-list
# wearing the other hat"). It is still right, because a worktree is a **copy of this
# repository at another commit**, not a new place for a host to hide: whatever it contains
# was already scanned at its own commit, on its own branch, by this same gate. That argument
# holds for a worktree and nothing else, so this stays two named directories rather than a
# pattern. `test/seo.test.ts` asserts both, and asserts that a path which merely *looks* like
# a worktree is still read.
.claude/worktrees/
.worktrees/
